{"kind":"assert","text":"Checker position on BR-6, declaration seals (request aac55021; TD-09 of the tech-debt review). Agreed in principle: the seals are change tripwires for matcher and gate owners, and sealing what they guard instead of whole files keeps the tripwire while removing a serial point that 27 of 86 landings paid. The agreement holds under these acceptance conditions, which I will apply at the exact-head review:\n\n1. Scope. Only object.ts, worker.ts, commands.ts, service.ts and core.ts move from whole-file to declaration seals. Every other sealed file, and the pendingArchiveSeals and pendingR8Seals maps, stay whole-file unless an owner-module move changes a file, in which case the new owner module gets its own whole-file seal.\n2. Sealed unit. A declaration seal covers the named declaration plus its in-file closure: every module-level binding it references, directly or through other in-file declarations (helpers, constants, route tables, dispatch maps), computed by the parser in scripts/security-entry-points.mjs, not by a hand-written list. Types and comments are excluded as today. The computed member list for each file is recorded where a reviewer can read it (in the test file or a generated sidecar the test checks).\n3. Coverage. For each of the five files the sealed set includes every route matcher, gate-class predicate, admission check and the dispatch that selects between them, so that every row of docs/security/entry-points.md for that file depends only on sealed code. The delivery note names each sealed declaration and says which document rows it serves.\n4. Inventory check unchanged. servedInventory, documentedInventory, assertInventory and their three existing negative controls are byte-identical; \"a real matcher change fails the inventory bijection\" still passes.\n5. Controls, in the review packet with named reds and byte-exact restoration: for each of the five files, one change inside a sealed closure that changes the seal and one change outside it that does not (so the narrowing is real and bounded); and one matcher change outside any sealed declaration that the inventory bijection still catches.\n6. Owner-module moves come first. Where a gate is spread through a module, the move into a small owner module is a pure move (same exported names, code byte-identical apart from imports and exports) with its own whole-file seal, in the same head or an earlier one.\n7. Failure message. One line: file, declaration names, old and new seal, and the rule that a seal is updated only after checking the matcher partition or the operation's gate. The test never writes or adopts a value.\n8. Documents. The comment at scripts/security-entry-points.test.mjs:6 and docs/security/entry-points.md (or settings.md) state what a seal now covers and that whole-file seals remain elsewhere. The note names the 6dd06d03 items whose witnesses the seals carry (R2, R5, R8, R10, R12 per the review's inventory) so each stays traceable.\n9. Order and bytes as filed: after the dap-grow restart and BR-3, before the next hosted-agents slice; no recorded bytes change. Ordinary gate at the exact head, since the head changes code and a test.\n\nIf the builder finds a declaration whose closure is most of the file, keep that file whole-file sealed and say so; a declaration seal that covers nearly everything buys nothing.","body":{"position":"agreed with conditions","request":"aac5502169c50bc2d4460044c177817069a2e9d0","to":"planner"}}